{"causes": ["The broader Investiture Controversy created a climate of escalating conflict between secular rulers and the ecclesiastical hierarchy across Europe.", "King Bolesław II the Bold's autocratic rule and alleged moral transgressions prompted strong opposition from the Polish clergy.", "Stanislaus's commitment to church reform and his role as Bishop of Kraków compelled him to challenge the king's authority and moral conduct."], "effects": ["King Bolesław II the Bold was forced into exile and subsequently died, leading to a temporary political crisis and dynastic shift in Poland.", "Stanislaus's martyrdom led to his posthumous veneration and eventual canonization in 1253, establishing him as a major patron saint of Poland.", "The narrative of Stanislaus's conflict with the king became a foundational myth for Polish national identity, symbolizing moral resistance against tyrannical power."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "investiture-controversy", "sourceName": "Investiture Controversy", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "stanislaus-of-szczepanw", "targetName": "Stanislaus of Szczepanów", "context": "The broader European Investiture Controversy provided the ideological framework for Stanislaus's conflict with King Bolesław II in the late 11th century."}, {"sourceSlug": "stanislaus-of-szczepanw", "sourceName": "Stanislaus of Szczepanów", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "krakow", "targetName": "Kraków", "context": "Stanislaus of Szczepanów served as Bishop of Kraków and was martyred within the city in 1079."}, {"sourceSlug": "boleslaw-ii-the-bold", "sourceName": "Bolesław II the Bold", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "stanislaus-of-szczepanw", "targetName": "Stanislaus of Szczepanów", "context": "King Bolesław II the Bold ordered or personally carried out the assassination of Bishop Stanislaus of Szczepanów in 1079."}, {"sourceSlug": "stanislaus-of-szczepanw", "sourceName": "Stanislaus of Szczepanów", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "polish-national-identity", "targetName": "Polish National Identity", "context": "The martyrdom of Stanislaus of Szczepanów profoundly influenced the development of Polish national identity as a symbol of moral and spiritual defiance."}, {"sourceSlug": "catholic-church", "sourceName": "Catholic Church", "verb": "CANONIZES", "targetSlug": "stanislaus-of-szczepanw", "targetName": "Stanislaus of Szczepanów", "context": "The Catholic Church formally canonized Stanislaus of Szczepanów in 1253, recognizing his martyrdom and establishing his cult."}], "places": [{"name": "Szczepanów, Poland", "role": "Birthplace"}, {"name": "Kraków, Poland", "role": "Episcopal See, Place of Martyrdom"}, {"name": "Wawel Cathedral, Poland", "role": "Burial site, Shrine"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Stanislaus of Szczepanów", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "stanislaus-of-szczepanów"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q351635", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stanislaus_of_Szczepan%C3%B3w"], "tags": ["Poland", "Religious Figures", "Europe", "comparative-religion", "doctrine-development", "cultural-diffusion"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Stanislaus of Szczepanów is remembered as a landmark person of the Medieval era, referenced in 0 Wikipedia language editions. Key lasting impacts include: Shaped religious practice and spiritual communities; Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Stanislaus's martyrdom directly led to the temporary downfall of King Bolesław II and solidified his role as a foundational figure in Polish national identity and moral resistance against state power for centuries.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-31T17:46:34Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30651659415", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Stanislaus of Szczepanów was a Polish Catholic prelate who served as Bishop of Kraków and was martyred by the Polish King Bolesław II the Bold. He is the patron saint of Poland.", "newValue": "Stanislaus of Szczepanów (born 1030, Szczepanów – died 1079, Kraków) was a pivotal figure in 11th-century Polish history, serving as the Bishop of Kraków and becoming an enduring national symbol through his martyrdom. His conflict with King Bolesław II the Bold, which culminated in his assassination, represented a critical clash between nascent state authority and ecclesiastical power in medieval Eastern Europe, echoing the broader Investiture Controversy. Stanislaus's defiance against royal overreach cemented his legacy far beyond his episcopal tenure.

Elevated to the episcopacy around 1072, Stanislaus quickly emerged as a forceful advocate for church reforms and moral conduct, challenging the often-brutal and autocratic rule of King Bolesław II. The precise nature of their dispute remains debated by historians, but it likely involved Stanislaus's condemnation of the king's alleged moral transgressions, including the abduction of a married noblewoman, and the excommunication of Bolesław II. This act of ecclesiastical censure, a bold assertion of spiritual authority over temporal power, directly led to Stanislaus's murder by the king's own hand (or by his command) on May 14, 1079, likely within the Church of St. Michael on Skałka in Kraków.

The immediate aftermath of Stanislaus's martyrdom was profound: Bolesław II was forced into exile, leading to a period of political instability and a temporary weakening of the Polish monarchy. Stanislaus quickly became venerated as a martyr, with his cult growing steadily. He was canonized by Pope Innocent IV in 1253, solidifying his status as one of Poland's principal patron saints. His story became a powerful narrative of moral resistance, inspiring generations of Poles and embodying the nation's struggle against tyranny and foreign domination."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-31T17:46:34Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30651659415", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Educated at in the 500 – 1500 CE", "type": "Person", "year": "500"}, {"title": "Influenced by in the 500 – 1500 CE", "type": "Person", "year": "500"}, {"title": "Born into in the 500 – 1500 CE", "type": "Person", "year": "500"}], "newValue": ["The broader Investiture Controversy created a climate of escalating conflict between secular rulers and the ecclesiastical hierarchy across Europe.", "King Bolesław II the Bold's autocratic rule and alleged moral transgressions prompted strong opposition from the Polish clergy.", "Stanislaus's commitment to church reform and his role as Bishop of Kraków compelled him to challenge the king's authority and moral conduct."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-31T17:46:34Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30651659415", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Founded through Stanislaus of Szczepanów's historical significance", "type": "Person"}, {"title": "Wrote through Stanislaus of Szczepanów's historical significance", "type": "Person"}, {"title": "Established through Stanislaus of Szczepanów's historical significance", "type": "Person"}, {"title": "Transformed through Stanislaus of Szczepanów's historical significance", "type": "Person"}], "newValue": ["King Bolesław II the Bold was forced into exile and subsequently died, le